# Security: Environment Secrets Documentation
# This file documents all sensitive environment variables and their security requirements
# DO NOT commit actual secret values to version control

## Critical Secrets (MUST be set before production deployment)

### Database Connection
# DATABASE_URL
# - Type: PostgreSQL connection URI
# - Format: postgresql://user:password@host:port/database
# - Security: Contains credentials - must be stored in secure vault
# - Required: YES (application will not start without it)
# - Rotation: Review credentials quarterly, rotate if exposed
# Example: postgresql://byimport_user:CHANGE_ME@prod-db.example.com:5432/by_import_production

### Anthropic API Key
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
# - Type: API token
# - Format: Bearer token provided by Anthropic
# - Security: Controls access to Claude AI models - highly sensitive
# - Required: YES (application will not start without it)
# - Rotation: Rotate immediately if exposed, follow Anthropic rotation policy
# - Rate Limits: Ensure proper rate limits configured per API key
# Example: sk-ant-v0-... (redacted for security)

## Configuration Secrets (Optional but recommended for production)

### Feature Flags with Security Implications
# ENABLE_AI_VALIDATION
# - Controls whether AI-based data validation is enabled
# - Default: true
# - Security Note: AI calls may expose data - verify compliance requirements

# ENABLE_AI_TRANSFORMATION
# - Controls whether AI-based data transformation is enabled
# - Default: true
# - Security Note: Same as above - verify data privacy compliance

# ENABLE_WEBHOOKS
# - Controls webhook functionality for event notifications
# - Default: true
# - Security Note: Webhooks may expose data outside system - require HTTPS and signature verification

# ENABLE_SCHEDULING
# - Controls background job scheduling
# - Default: true
# - Security Note: Ensure only trusted jobs are scheduled

### Processing Configuration (affects data security)
# BATCH_SIZE
# - Number of records processed in single batch
# - Default: 1000
# - Security Note: Larger batches may impact memory and security auditing

# MAX_RETRIES
# - Number of retry attempts on failure
# - Default: 3
# - Security Note: Excessive retries could expose sensitive data in logs

# TIMEOUT_MS
# - Processing timeout in milliseconds
# - Default: 300000 (5 minutes)
# - Security Note: Too short may cause incomplete processing; too long may impact responsiveness

### File Upload Configuration (critical for data security)
# MAX_FILE_SIZE
# - Maximum allowed file size in bytes
# - Default: 104857600 (100MB)
# - Security Note: Prevent DoS attacks via large file uploads

# UPLOAD_DIR
# - Directory where uploaded files are stored
# - Default: ./uploads
# - Security Note: Ensure directory has proper permissions (700), separate from source code
# - Recommendation: Use /var/lib/by-import/uploads on production

# TEMP_DIR
# - Directory for temporary processing files
# - Default: ./temp
# - Security Note: Ensure directory is on secure partition, monitor disk space
# - Cleanup: Configure cron job to clean files older than 24 hours

## Environment Variable Security Checklist

### Before Production Deployment
- [ ] DATABASE_URL points to production database with strong password
- [ ] ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is from production Anthropic account
- [ ] NODE_ENV is set to 'production'
- [ ] LOG_LEVEL is set to 'warn' or 'error' (not 'debug')
- [ ] UPLOAD_DIR and TEMP_DIR are on separate, encrypted partitions
- [ ] All environment variables are set via secure mechanism (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, etc.)
- [ ] No .env files committed to version control
- [ ] Environment variables are rotated regularly per company policy
- [ ] Access to environment variables is logged and audited

### Secret Rotation Policy
1. Database credentials: Quarterly or on employee departure
2. API keys: Quarterly or if exposed
3. Application secrets: Semi-annually or per compliance requirements

### Handling Exposed Secrets
1. Immediately revoke exposed credentials
2. Generate new secrets from secure source
3. Deploy new values using CI/CD pipeline
4. Review logs for unauthorized access during exposure window
5. Notify security team and conduct incident review

### Environment-Specific Guidelines
- **Development**: May use test credentials, local files acceptable
- **Staging**: Same security level as production, no real customer data
- **Production**: All secrets must be from secure vault, encrypted in transit and at rest

## Implementation Notes

### Using AWS Secrets Manager
```bash
aws secretsmanager create-secret --name by-import/prod \
  --secret-string '{"DATABASE_URL":"...","ANTHROPIC_API_KEY":"..."}'
```

### Using HashiCorp Vault
```bash
vault kv put secret/by-import/prod DATABASE_URL=... ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...
```

### Using .env.local (Development Only)
- Create .env.local from .env.example
- Fill in development credentials
- Never commit to version control
- Add .env.local to .gitignore

## Monitoring and Alerts

Set up alerts for:
- Frequent API key usage patterns (indication of abuse)
- Unusual database query patterns
- Failed authentication attempts
- Secrets Manager access attempts
- Environment variable modifications in production

## References
- OWASP: Secrets Management
- CWE-798: Use of Hard-Coded Credentials
- NIST: Guidelines for Managing and Securing Cryptographic Keys
